        "pn_1": {
          "type": "panel",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "ONE",
            "title": "<strong>Cold-pressed and organic plant oils</strong>",
            "body": "<p>The base of every product is cold-pressed and organic plant oils. Cold pressing avoids the heat and solvents of refining, which means it preserves the bioactive compounds that give a plant oil its character: carotenoids, tocopherols, phytosterols and the phenolics that refined oils tend to lose. The research credits these compounds with antioxidant and skin-conditioning qualities, and there is a practical bonus too, since a higher phenolic content also improves the oil's oxidative stability, so the oil stays fresh for longer.</p><p>We choose these oils for what is in them. The particular oils change with the skin type, but the reasoning does not.</p>",
            "image_alt": ""
          }
        },
        "pn_2": {
          "type": "panel",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "TWO",
            "title": "<strong>The microbiome, and what we do about it</strong>",
            "body": "<p>Two ingredients are chosen with the skin's microbiome in mind. <a href=\"/blogs/ingredients/inulin\">Inulin</a> is a plant-derived prebiotic, food for the microbes already on your skin, with selective feeding shown in the laboratory but not yet on human skin. A <a href=\"/blogs/ingredients/chlorella-vulgaris-extract\">chlorella extract</a> has supplier evidence, at the level its maker tested, for holding back the more troublesome species while sparing the helpful ones.</p><p>Much of what a moisturiser can do for the microbiome comes from what it leaves out, and nothing in the formula is there to strip or sterilise. Once a formula is made, the final step is to adjust it to slightly acidic (pH 5 to 5.5), the level the skin's resident microbes prefer. Of everything here, that adjustment has the strongest published evidence behind it. There is more on the <a href=\"/pages/the-skin-microbiome\">skin microbiome page</a>.</p>",
            "image_alt": ""
          }
        },
        "pn_3": {
          "type": "panel",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "THREE",
            "title": "<strong>Atlantic seaweed</strong>",
            "body": "<p>One ingredient links the range to its home. <a href=\"/blogs/ingredients/bladder-wrack-fucus-vesiculosus-extract\">Bladderwrack</a>, <em>Fucus vesiculosus</em>, is the same Atlantic seaweed species you find on the rocks along the north Devon coast. Ours is a cold infusion in sunflower oil, made in the UK. Carried in oil, it is the oil-soluble part, chiefly fucoxanthin, that comes across, and the research credits fucoxanthin with modest antioxidant activity.</p>",
            "image_alt": "Bladderwrack on wet rock at low tide"
          }
        },
        "b_leaveout": {
          "type": "band",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What we leave out",
            "body": "<p>We leave out synthetic fragrance entirely, and nothing hides behind a \"parfum\" or \"fragrance\" catch-all. Fragrance ingredients are among the more common triggers for irritation and allergy, which matters most for sensitive skin, so the one aromatic in the range is a single named essential oil at a tenth of a percent, explained just below. Every ingredient we use is listed individually, most abundant first.</p>",
            "caveat": "",
            "variant": "plain",
            "set_piece": "none",
            "chips": "",
            "mark_1": "",
            "mark_2": "",
            "mark_3": "",
